This version lets a helper build the list from left to right. It receives the previous node, creates the current node, and then builds the rest of the list through `next`.

For example, with `[2, 5, 8]`, the helper first creates `2`, then `5` with `prev` set to `2`, and finally `8` with `prev` set to `5`. The recursive call at the end returns `nullptr` for the last node's `next` pointer.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution
{
    ListNode* build(const vector<int>& arr, int index, ListNode* previous)
    {
        if (index == static_cast<int>(arr.size()))
        {
            return nullptr;
        }

        ListNode* current = new ListNode(arr[index], previous, nullptr);
        current->next = build(arr, index + 1, current);
        return current;
    }

public:
    ListNode *arrayToDoublyLinkedList(vector<int> &arr) {
        return build(arr, 0, nullptr);
    }
};
```

The empty array stops at the first helper call and returns `nullptr`. This does not modify the input vector, but recursion uses call stack space in addition to the nodes.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the list and `O(n)` recursive call stack.

A small dummy node makes the loop simpler because every real node is added after an existing node. At the end, the dummy node is skipped and the first real node is returned.

The first real node gets `prev = null`, while every later node points back to the node added just before it. The dummy node is only temporary and is not part of the returned list.

```java
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public ListNode arrayToDoublyLinkedList(List<Integer> arr) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode tail = dummy;

        for (int value : arr) {
            ListNode newNode = new ListNode(value);
            newNode.prev = tail == dummy ? null : tail;
            tail.next = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

For an empty list, `dummy.next` is already `null`, so no special loop handling is needed. The array remains unchanged.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the nodes, plus one temporary dummy node.

Here the nodes are created first and connected in a separate pass. Keeping the nodes in a list makes the neighbor positions easy to access: node `i` gets node `i - 1` as `prev` and node `i + 1` as `next`.

This uses more temporary memory than linking while creating nodes, but the two jobs are clearly separated. The first and last nodes are handled by checking their positions.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public ListNode arrayToDoublyLinkedList(List<Integer> arr) {
        if (arr.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }

        List<ListNode> nodes = new ArrayList<>(arr.size());

        for (int value : arr) {
            nodes.add(new ListNode(value));
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < nodes.size(); i++) {
            ListNode current = nodes.get(i);
            current.prev = i == 0 ? null : nodes.get(i - 1);
            current.next = i + 1 == nodes.size() ? null : nodes.get(i + 1);
        }

        return nodes.get(0);
    }
}
```

The empty input check returns `null` before trying to access the first node. The original `List<Integer>` is not modified.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the nodes and the temporary Java list.

